// simd_probe.kama — the kernel `check-simd-native.sh` and `check-simd-wasm.sh` compile to prove that
// kama's math actually vectorizes. NOT a harness fixture: `tests/support/` is outside run_tests.sh's
// four globs, so nothing here runs as a test (check-fixture-reach.sh still requires it to analyze
// clean). It is a probe, and its SHAPE is the whole point.
//
// ⚠️ Three ways to write this so it reports "no SIMD" against a perfectly vectorized compiler — all
// three were in the probe that produced this repo's earlier FALSE finding (ROADMAP_DETAIL.md §2):
//
//   1. NEVER measure a single op in its own function. AAPCS64's HFA rule passes `struct{float x,y,z,w}`
//      in s0-s3 as four SEPARATE registers, so an isolated `a + b` is scalar for ABI reasons that have
//      nothing to do with the vectorizer. Measure a LOOP OVER MEMORY.
//   2. NEVER let the kernel be provably dead. File-static arrays that nothing seeds and nothing reads
//      back get constant-folded and dead-stored; three of five kernels in the original probe compiled
//      to a bare `ret`. Here the buffers arrive as PARAMETERS and the kernel is `expose`d
//      (KAMA_EXPORT -> visibility("default"),used), so neither the optimizer nor --gc-sections can
//      delete it and the compiler cannot see the values.
//   3. The grep is the guards' problem, not this file's, but it belongs with the other two: Apple's
//      assembler writes `fadd.4s v0, v0, v1`, NOT `fadd v0.4s`.
//
// `main` seeds real data and checks the result, so a build of this probe is also a correctness test
// of the thing being measured — a vectorized kernel that computes the wrong answer fails here rather
// than passing a grep.
import { std::math::Vec4, std::collections::FixedArray, std::collections::View };

// THE KERNEL. Elementwise `dst[i] = a[i] + b[i]` over borrowed memory — the exact shape measured in
// ROADMAP_DETAIL.md §2, which compiles to a 16-byte-per-iteration NEON loop (`fadd.4s`) on native
// and, with -msimd128, to `f32x4.add` on wasm. The View bounds checks and the operator's
// compound-literal convention all disappear at -O3.
expose fn void simdProbeAddAll(View<Vec4> dst, View<Vec4> a, View<Vec4> b) {
    isize n = dst.length();
    isize i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        dst[i] = a[i] + b[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
}

fn int32 main() {
    isize n = 256;
    FixedArray<Vec4> a = FixedArray.make(size: n);
    FixedArray<Vec4> b = FixedArray.make(size: n);
    FixedArray<Vec4> c = FixedArray.make(size: n);

    isize i = 0;
    while (i < n) {
        float32 f = cast<float32>(i);
        a[i] = Vec4.splat(s: f);
        b[i] = Vec4.splat(s: 1.0f32);
        i = i + 1;
    }

    borrow c.view() as vc {
        borrow a.view() as va {
            borrow b.view() as vb {
                simdProbeAddAll(dst: vc, a: va, b: vb);
            }
        }
    }

    // Read the result back so the kernel cannot be elided, and check it: lane w of element 255 is 256.
    if (c[255].w != 256.0f32) { return 1; }
    if (c[0].x   != 1.0f32)   { return 2; }
    return 0;
}
